-- Refinement types (widening slice 12, §2.14). A documented scope boundary
-- (specs/lang/refinement-types.md's own Limitations section).
--
-- NARROWED 2026-07-25: refinement checking is no longer "direct calls only".
-- Two higher-order shapes ARE checked now -- a call through a REFINED callback
-- type (`f : ({Int | _ >= 0}) -> Int`), and a call through a local alias
-- (`let g = take_n` then `g(-3)`). This witness still passes because it uses
-- neither: the callback type `Int -> Int` is unrefined and `x` is unrefined,
-- so nothing at the call site states the requirement. Catching THIS shape
-- needs contravariant subtyping at the pass site, or inferring `apply`'s own
-- requirement from its body -- both still open. `apply(take_n, -3)` calls `take_n` indirectly (through the `f`
-- parameter), so refine_check never associates the argument `-3` with
-- `take_n`'s `{Int | _ >= 0}` precondition -- `--check` accepts even though
-- the same literal at a DIRECT `take_n(-3)` call is rejected (reject/t71).
-- This is an honest scope boundary, not unsoundness: no runtime check is
-- promised either (core-march-types.md §2.14 states the erasure explicitly),
-- so nothing here is unsafe -- just unverified. NOT a bug; do not "fix" by
-- making this reject without reading §2.14's scope note first.
mod M do
  fn take_n(n : {Int | _ >= 0}) : Int do n end
  fn apply(f : Int -> Int, x : Int) : Int do f(x) end
  fn main() do
    println(int_to_string(apply(take_n, -3)))
  end
end
